# React Patterns

## Component Structure

```typescript
// 1. Imports (React, external, internal, types)
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { Button } from '@/components/ui';
import { useData } from '@/hooks/useData';
import type { Customer } from '@/types';

// 2. Types/Interfaces
interface Props {
  customerId: string;
  onSave: (data: Customer) => void;
}

// 3. Component (named export)
export function CustomerForm({ customerId, onSave }: Props) {
  // 4. Hooks first
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const { data, error } = useData(`/customers/${customerId}`);

  // 5. Effects
  useEffect(() => {
    // Effect logic
  }, [customerId]);

  // 6. Handlers
  const handleSubmit = async () => {
    // Handler logic
  };

  // 7. Early returns for loading/error states
  if (loading) return <LoadingSpinner />;
  if (error) return <ErrorMessage error={error} />;

  // 8. Main render
  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      {/* JSX */}
    </form>
  );
}
```

## Hooks Best Practices

- Extract reusable logic to custom hooks in `/src/hooks/`
- Use existing hooks: `useData`, `useForm` from the project
- Prefer `useMemo` and `useCallback` for expensive operations
- Clean up subscriptions/timers in useEffect return

## State Management

- Local state for UI-only concerns
- React Query (via `useData`) for server state
- Lift state up only when needed by siblings

## Error Handling

- Wrap pages in `ErrorBoundary` component
- Use try/catch in async handlers
- Display user-friendly error messages

## Performance

- Lazy load pages with `React.lazy()`
- Memoize expensive computations
- Avoid inline function definitions in render
- Use pagination for large lists (see `Pagination` component)

## Existing UI Components

Use components from `src/components/ui.tsx`:

- Button, Input, Select, Card
- Table, Modal, Toast
- See @src/components/ui.tsx for full list
